# Baked Trust Page artifacts (Phase I ingestion, ADR 0046) are reproducible,
# byte-exact outputs of `pnpm bake:trust-index`. They must never be line-ending-
# normalized on checkout, or the committed bytes would differ from a fresh emit on
# Windows (CRLF) vs. Linux/macOS (LF) and the reproducibility gate would fail. Pin
# them to LF on every platform so the working tree always matches the emit.
# These are committed under apps/web/public/trust so deploy-web.yml serves them
# same-origin at calllint.com/trust/… (ADR 0046 §4).
apps/web/public/trust/** text eol=lf

# The retained Official MCP Registry snapshot (ADR 0038 §1) is the committed input
# the bake re-runs from. Pin it to LF too so the ingestion input is byte-identical
# across platforms (the reproducibility gate re-bakes from these exact bytes).
packages/trust-index/snapshots/** text eol=lf

# Offline audit artifacts (Gate A coverage audit, Gate B calibration gate) are
# byte-exact projections regenerated by `pnpm audit:coverage:write` /
# `pnpm audit:calibration:write`. Their drift `--check` compares committed bytes to a
# fresh render, so they must stay LF on every platform (a CRLF Windows checkout would
# false-fail the check). Pin them to LF (ADR 0053 §6; same discipline as the bake).
packages/resolver/audit/** text eol=lf
packages/trust-index/calibration/** text eol=lf

# The Safe Search bundled committed index (ADR 0055 §4) is a byte-copy of the baked
# apps/web/public/trust/lookup-index.json, imported as a module and pinned byte-identical
# by committed-lookup-drift.test.ts. The baked original is eol=lf (above), so this copy
# must be too — otherwise a CRLF Windows checkout diverges and the anti-drift test false-fails.
packages/calllint-mcp/src/data/** text eol=lf

# The Phase 2.4 Safe-install SERVED acquisition surface (ADR 0056; PR 2.4-3) is a
# reproducible, byte-exact projection of the same committed inputs, emitted by
# `pnpm bake:trust-index` into the site root and pinned byte-identical by
# safe-install/committed-install-tree.test.ts. Same discipline as apps/web/public/trust
# above: pin to LF on every platform, or a CRLF Windows checkout would diverge from the
# LF emit and the reproducibility gate would false-fail on Windows only. `.well-known`
# is pinned narrowly to the CallLint manifest so a foreign security.txt is untouched.
apps/web/public/install/** text eol=lf
apps/web/public/.well-known/calllint.json text eol=lf

# The Workstream P presentation content plane (ADR 0058 §2; PR P-2) is CONFIGURATION,
# not a generated artifact — but its bytes are hashed. `presentationDigest` over this
# document is recorded in artifacts/phase-2.4/presentation-lock.json, whose default
# `--check` mode compares committed bytes to a fresh run, so a CRLF Windows checkout
# would move the digest and false-fail the lock on Windows only. Same trap as the bake:
# pin to LF on every platform. (Hand-edited files are exactly where this is easiest to
# forget, because nothing regenerates them to reveal the mismatch.)
apps/web/content/** text eol=lf

# The Workstream P L0 token plane (ADR 0058 §4; PR P-4, served by P-4b). Hand-authored
# CSS. Its bytes ARE measured: presentation-lock.json pins every `:root` value in this
# file against apps/web/public/styles.css byte-for-byte, and a CRLF Windows checkout would
# put a stray \r inside the last declaration of each line-terminated value and false-fail
# the drift pin on Windows only. `ci:local` structurally cannot catch that — the 3-OS
# matrix is the only gate that sees it (the trap that broke #240).
apps/web/styles/** text eol=lf

# The SERVED copy of that plane (PR P-4b). `sync-assets.mjs` copies apps/web/styles/
# tokens.css to apps/web/public/styles/tokens.css, which is the byte a visitor receives,
# and the lock BYTE-COMPARES the two. The source above is eol=lf, so this copy must be
# too — otherwise a CRLF Windows checkout makes an exact copy compare unequal to its own
# source and the lock false-fails on Windows only. Pinning one side and not the other is
# precisely how that trap gets built.
apps/web/public/styles/** text eol=lf

# The copy-only assist (ADR 0059 §4) is the same source/served split as tokens.css above,
# and needs both sides pinned for the same reason: `sync-assets.mjs` copies apps/web/
# scripts/install-copy.js to apps/web/public/scripts/install-copy.js, and render.test.ts
# BYTE-COMPARES the two. Leave the authored side unpinned and a Windows checkout with
# core.autocrlf=true hands it CRLF while the served copy stays LF, so an exact copy
# compares unequal to its own source on windows-latest only — invisible to `ci:local`.
# All 19 install pages reference the served path, so this is a live 200-or-404 byte.
apps/web/scripts/** text eol=lf
apps/web/public/scripts/** text eol=lf

# The Phase 2.4 gate evidence artifacts (new14 §gates; PR 2.4-9) are byte-stable
# projections regenerated by `pnpm eval:phase-2.4:write` / `:dogfood:write`. Their
# default `--check` mode compares COMMITTED bytes to a fresh render, so a CRLF Windows
# checkout would false-fail the drift check on Windows only — the same trap the bake
# hit. Pin them to LF on every platform. `five-second-panel-store.json` is
# HUMAN-written rather than rendered, but is pinned too so its digest is stable.
artifacts/phase-2.4/** text eol=lf

# The Workstream R Batch-0 reality-audit artifacts (new15 R-0; ADR 0061). Nothing
# drift-checks these bytes YET, so nothing fails today — the pin is added in the same
# batch that creates the directory precisely BECAUSE of that. Every later R batch reads
# these six artifacts, and the first one to byte-compare them would otherwise introduce
# the check and the missing pin together, where a miss surfaces on windows-latest ALONE
# (CRLF checkout) and `ci:local` structurally cannot see it. That is the trap #240 hit.
# Pin the directory before anything measures it; the line costs nothing until it doesn't.
artifacts/adoption-index-v1/** text eol=lf

# The canonical adoption-graph migrations (new15 R-1; ADR 0061 §10.2). These are the one
# place in the repo where a source file's BYTES are a durable identifier: `loadMigrations`
# records `sha256(sql)` into `schema_migrations`, and `applyMigrations` refuses to run when
# a recorded digest no longer matches the file. A CRLF checkout therefore does not merely
# reformat a file, it CHANGES ITS IDENTITY — the same DDL digests 4ac16f96… at LF and
# 7886b43b… at CRLF, so a store created on Linux and opened on Windows would report the
# migration as "modified after it was applied" and refuse to open.
#
# Measured, not anticipated: R-2 pinned the shipped migration's digest to its value (the
# harness gap control #11 exposed), and windows-latest went red ALONE while ubuntu and
# macOS passed — `git check-attr text` on this directory reported `unspecified`. That is
# the #240 trap, and `ci:local` cannot see it on an LF working copy by construction.
packages/adoption-index/migrations/** text eol=lf

# The Workstream M MCP 2026-07-28 readiness audit (new16-new17 §4.2; M26-0). Same reasoning as
# artifacts/adoption-index-v1 at R-0: nothing drift-checks these bytes YET, so nothing fails
# today — the pin is added in the batch that CREATES the directory precisely because of that.
# `finality-status.json` is the evidence base every later M batch reads (INV-M5), and M26-5 will
# digest-lock vendored spec bytes against it. The first batch to byte-compare or hash these files
# would otherwise introduce the check and the missing pin together, where a miss surfaces on
# windows-latest ALONE (CRLF checkout) and `ci:local` structurally cannot see it — the #240 trap.
artifacts/mcp-2026-07-28/** text eol=lf

# The Workstream R worker deployment units (new15 R-9; ADR 0061 §8.5). Unlike every pin
# above, nothing in this repo hashes these bytes — the consumer is systemd on the worker
# host, and it is NOT tolerant: a CRLF checkout makes the last token of every line carry a
# trailing \r, so `ExecStart=/usr/bin/pnpm prune:cas` becomes a request to run a binary
# whose name ends in \r, and `Environment="CAS_RETENTION_DAYS=90"` yields the value `90\r`.
# The failure lands at deploy time on the host, where no CI job of ours is watching, which
# makes it strictly worse than the windows-latest-only false-fails the pins above prevent.
# `core.autocrlf` is false on the authoring machine, so the hazard is invisible here and
# arrives with the first contributor running Git for Windows defaults. Pin the directory
# with the batch that creates it, exactly as artifacts/adoption-index-v1 did at R-0.
deploy/** text eol=lf

# The T0 trajectory-readiness audit (new16 §T0; ADR 0062). Same reasoning as
# artifacts/mcp-2026-07-28 above: nothing hashes these bytes YET, so nothing fails today, and the
# pin is added by the batch that CREATES the directory precisely so a later batch cannot introduce
# a byte-comparison and a missing pin together — a miss that surfaces on windows-latest ALONE and
# that `ci:local` structurally cannot see on an LF working copy.
#
# Unlike every pin above, this one has NO row in `SERVED_SUBTREES` (scripts/phase-2.4-gates.ts) and
# no guard test, so by that table's own docblock it is itself unguarded: deleting this line would
# fail nothing. Recorded rather than hidden. These are prose chapters with no source/served split to
# byte-compare, so a guard would mean inventing a consumer for the sake of guarding the pin.
artifacts/trajectory-v0/** text eol=lf

# The Gate S0 carried open items (Workstream S; `artifacts/gate-s0/open-items.md`).
#
# Same reasoning as artifacts/mcp-2026-07-28 above, with one difference that makes the pin land
# earlier rather than later: the batch that creates this directory ALSO creates its first reader
# (`tests/invariants/gate-s0-claims.invariants.test.ts`), so unlike every unguarded pin above there
# is a consumer from day one. That reader normalizes CRLF at its own boundary anyway — per ADR 0064
# §6.2, which was paid for by a gate that sliced un-normalized source and then blamed the wrong
# method — so the pin is not what keeps it green.
#
# It is here because the reader also reads `scripts/gate-s0.ts`, `package.json` and
# `.github/workflows/trust-ingest.yml`, all `unspecified`, and normalization at the reader is a
# property of the reader, not of these bytes. A second consumer that byte-compares or hashes this
# artifact — the shape every other artifact directory eventually grew — would otherwise introduce
# the check and the missing pin together, where the miss surfaces on windows-latest ALONE and
# `ci:local` structurally cannot see it (the #240 trap).
artifacts/gate-s0/** text eol=lf

# The Gate S1 carried open items (`artifacts/gate-s1/open-items.md`).
#
# Same reasoning as the Gate S0 pin directly above, and added in the same batch as its reader
# (`tests/invariants/gate-s1-claims.invariants.test.ts`) for the same reason: a consumer from day
# one, so the pin is not deferred to whichever later batch introduces a byte-comparing reader and
# discovers the missing pin only on windows-latest.
#
# One difference worth naming, because it is why this artifact exists at all: S1's status previously
# lived ONLY in `docs/new15-execution-status.md`, which is gitignored — so unlike S0, whose record
# was merely unread, S1's threshold had no tracked record and no guard whatsoever while the cohort
# crossed it (19 → 100 → 150). A pin on bytes that exist is the cheap half of the fix; the reader is
# the other half.
artifacts/gate-s1/** text eol=lf

# The vendored MCP 2026-07-28 specification bytes (M26-5; third_party/mcp-spec/2026-07-28/).
#
# This pin differs from every other one above in that it is NOT unguarded. The digest lock in
# `SOURCE.json` is read by `tests/invariants/mcp-spec-vendor.invariants.test.ts`, so a CRLF checkout
# reds the gate twice over: the sha256 moves, and a dedicated assertion counts \r bytes and names
# the file. `deploy/**` and `artifacts/trajectory-v0/**` are pins that nothing hashes; this one has a
# consumer, which is what O-M1 means by "a gate with no evidence artifact is not a gate".
#
# These bytes must stay verbatim upstream copies — that is the entire value of vendoring them. Any
# transformation on checkout, line endings included, makes the local file something upstream never
# published while leaving it looking authentic.
third_party/** text eol=lf

# The distribution SSOT's 31 projections (`pnpm gen:distribution`, gated by
# `pnpm check:distribution-drift`). Same trap as every pin above, and this one was MEASURED rather
# than anticipated: the drift gate byte-compares each generated file against a fresh emit, the
# generator writes '\n', and windows-latest checks out with core.autocrlf=true. 13 of the 31 came
# back "differs from the SSOT projection" with the on-disk file LARGER by exactly its own line
# count — 197 bytes over 197 lines, 912 over 912, 13 for 13. That arithmetic is the whole diagnosis:
# one stray \r per line, no content difference at all.
#
# WHY ALL 31 AND NOT THE 13 THAT FAILED. The 13 are not a property of the files; they are a property
# of which files that run happened to rewrite. Every one of the 31 is emitted with LF and compared
# byte-for-byte, so each is one content change away from being the next to red. Pinning the failures
# only would leave 18 loaded traps and make the next occurrence look like a new bug.
#
# `apps/web/public/harnesses/**` is 21 tracked files and all 21 are projections, so the directory
# glob has no collateral. The two `artifacts/` globs do reach hand-written docs; that is harmless
# (LF is what this repo commits anyway) and it also catches
# `artifacts/authority-distribution-closure/security-semantic-diff.json`, which is itself a
# `--check` byte-compared artifact and belongs pinned on its own merits.
#
# `ci:local` on one machine structurally cannot see any of this — the 3-OS matrix is the only
# reader, the same way it was for apps/web/styles (#240).
apps/web/public/harnesses/** text eol=lf
apps/web/public/agent-surfaces.json text eol=lf
apps/web/public/agent-discovery-index.json text eol=lf
apps/web/public/agent-instructions.md text eol=lf
apps/web/public/llms.txt text eol=lf
apps/web/public/llms-full.txt text eol=lf
apps/web/public/_redirects text eol=lf
scripts/distribution-sources.json text eol=lf
artifacts/submissions/** text eol=lf
artifacts/authority-distribution-closure/** text eol=lf

# The generator's INPUTS, and the half of this pin that pinning the outputs alone made WORSE.
#
# MEASURED, in the clone built to verify the block above. With only the outputs pinned, the same
# `core.autocrlf=true` clone went from 13 drifted files to EIGHTEEN — every harness page, each one
# now SMALLER than expected by 135–156 bytes instead of larger. The generator writes
# `Buffer.from(content, 'utf8')` and never touches os.EOL, so the \r could only come from what it
# reads: `host-page.hbs` had been checked out with 220 CRLF, and Handlebars copies the template's
# bytes into its output verbatim. Pinning the product and not the source means the EXPECTED bytes
# carry \r while the on-disk bytes no longer do — the comparison inverts and fails wider.
#
# That is not a new hazard; it is the one the tokens.css pin above already records ("Pinning one
# side and not the other is precisely how that trap gets built"), reached from the other direction.
# Both sides of a byte-compare must be pinned or neither is.
#
# distribution-surfaces.json is the SSOT the whole plane projects from, and its schema sits beside
# it; both are read, hashed, and diffed. `scripts/templates/**` is pinned as a directory because a
# second template would inherit the same trap silently.
#
# `project-facts.json` is the generator's fourth input and is deliberately NOT pinned here. It is
# consumed through `JSON.parse` and read field-by-field (`facts.install`, `facts.stableVersion`), so
# CRLF is eaten as JSON whitespace and no \r can reach a projection's bytes — checked, not assumed.
# It has its own byte-level reader in `check:public-copy` §11c; pinning it belongs to that gate's
# scope, not to this failure's.
scripts/templates/** text eol=lf
apps/web/data/distribution-surfaces.json text eol=lf
apps/web/data/distribution-surfaces.schema.json text eol=lf
